{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-213789","model_call":21,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"black americans\" \"african americans\" \"black people\") \"kidney disease\" (+ \"higher risk\" \"greater risk\" \"more likely\") (+ diabetes hypertension \"high blood pressure\"))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"black americans\" \"african americans\" \"black people\") \"kidney disease\" (+ \"higher risk\" \"greater risk\" \"more likely\") (+ diabetes hypertension \"high blood pressure\"))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1740835569#3_3567314416","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3663","rank":1,"snippet":"Change the Face of Kidney Disease Can anyone get kidney disease? Why are African Americans at greater risk for kidney disease? How does access to healthcare play a role? What to do? \" , \"segment\": \"Join Now Can anyone get kidney disease? Yes. Anyone can get kidney disease at any age. But some people are more likely than others to get it, including African Americans. Having diabetes, high blood pressure, a family history of chronic kidney disease, and being 60 years or older also increases the risk for kidney disease. The more risk factors you have, the more likely you are to get kidney disease. Why are African Americans at <cover>greater risk for kidney disease? African Americans have more diabetes </cover>and high blood pressure than other Americans. Having diabetes or high blood pressure can lead to kidney disease and kidney failure. Heart and blood vessel disease also plays a major role among African Americans.\" , \"start_char\": 1643.000000 , \"title\": \"African Americans and Kidney Disease | National Kidney Foundation\" , \"url\": \"https://www.kidney.org/atoz/content/AfricanAmericans-KD\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1740835569#3_3567314416","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":1109},"model_calls":21,"document_judgments":17,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-213789","model_call":21,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3663"},"model_calls":21,"document_judgments":17,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-213789","model_call":21,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3663"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q3663"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_10_661960532#1_1236808879","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3663","rank":2,"snippet":"between African-Americans and white Americans. \" , \"segment\": \"It means overcoming disparities in health care. It means investments targeted to the health of black Americans. And the evidence so far indicates that these investments will pay health dividends not just for racial minorities, but for everyone. Yet we're closer to the beginning of the fight than to the end. Some numbers: Diabetes is 60% more common in black Americans than in white Americans. Blacks are up to 2.5 times more likely to suffer a limb amputation and up to 5.6 times <cover>more likely to suffer kidney disease than other people with diabetes. African-Americans </cover>are three times more likely to die of asthma than white Americans. Deaths from lung scarring -- sarcoidosis -- are 16 times more common among blacks than among whites. The disease recently killed former NFL star Reggie White at age 43.\" , \"start_char\": 1008.000000 , \"title\": \"Why 7 Deadly Diseases Strike Blacks Most\" , \"url\": \"http://www.webmd.com/hypertension-high-blood-pressure/features/why-7-deadly-diseases-strike-blacks-most\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1740835569#3_3567314416","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":9},"model_calls":21,"document_judgments":17,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
